Reads one local through a pointer to the one declared next to it, which is
undefined behaviour rather than a property of C.

gcc fails this file exactly where the IR backend does - both return 3 - so the
legacy backend is the outlier, not the one that is right. It passes there
because it emits every declared local and lays them out in declaration order;
the IR backend agrees about the order and removes the local, since a variable
whose address is never taken and whose value is never read is a dead store to a
dead alloca.

Skipped rather than muted: keeping a provably dead local alive so that an
out-of-bounds read finds it would be a deoptimization in the service of
undefined behaviour, so there is no bug here for anyone to fix and nothing to
flag the day it starts passing. See section 10 of docs/ir-codegen-design.md.
